{"input": "Summarize the discussion about the working design of the remote control.", "answers": ["The industrial designer described the product as a portable device with some energy source, an interface which included some pressing buttons and keys, a processor, some infrared source which could send the information to the main device, and different encryption codes for different TVs. Since the Marketing and the user interface designer mentioned speech recognition, the industrial designer also took that into account. All these components would cost 5 Euros or even less than that. As for gesture recognition and automatic power control, the group gave them up due to price issues. At the end of the meeting, the group also talked about how to enable the users to find the controller."], "length": 7341, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The discussion about the working design of the remote control was led by Industrial Designer Ramaro, who explained that the basic function of the remote control is to send messages to the main device, such as a TV. He outlined the necessary components, including a portable device, energy source (battery), user interface (keypad and buttons), digital signal processing chip, infrared LED source, and switch in the main device to perform particular operations. The team discussed the need for encryption codes to work with different TV brands and the possibility of incorporating speech recognition and gesture recognition features, but ultimately decided to focus on a simple design with a limited number of functions and a budget of around 12 Euros."}
{"input": "What did the group talk about the touch screen under the limitation of the budget?", "answers": ["The group found that if they would use the touch screen as well as the voice recognition technique, the cost would up to 17 euros. On the other hand, in order to guarantee the one hundred percent profit, the price would be more than 30, which would risk losing potential consumers. Provided that they would control the cost within 12.5 euros, they would have to cut off some functions, for example, voice recognition. It was really hard to balance between the popular tastes, the cost and the quality."], "length": 7881, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The group discussed the touch screen feature, but ultimately decided to omit it in favor of voice recognition due to the high cost of production, estimated to be around 17.50 Euros per remote, which would push the price up to 25 Euros, exceeding their target of being under 12.50 Euros. They considered the touch screen to be a luxury feature that would make the remote control a high-end product, but decided to prioritize voice recognition as a more cost-effective and marketable feature."}
{"input": "What was the general discussion?", "answers": ["The group discussed recognition results generated for 20 minutes of close-talking microphone data. Recognition performance was very good, indicating promising results for forced alignment procedures and the ability to analyze other important signal information, e.g. prosody and overlapping speech. It was decided that close-talking data should be downsampled and fed to the SRI recognizer to compare recognition performance, and that data from the far-field microphones should be tested on the recognizer as soon as possible. The group also discussed recording setup and equipment issues. "], "length": 12783, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The general discussion revolved around the progress of a speech recognition project, including the results of a recent meeting transcription, the performance of a new recognizer, and the potential for adapting the language models to improve recognition accuracy. The team also discussed the logistics of recording meetings, including the use of wireless microphones, the need for a third board to accommodate more channels, and the possibility of using a hand-held microphone to pass around to different speakers. Additionally, they touched on the topic of downsampling and the potential for using a different sampling rate, and discussed the progress of the transcription team, who were working to transcribe 11 hours of audio by the end of the month."}
{"input": "Summarize the Project Manager's perspective on the prototype.", "answers": ["The Project Manager thought the prototype was basically the same with the former one, and the thickness was not suitable for the target customers. Then the LCD screen might be covered when held by hands, so the users couldn't figure out what happened on the screen. Therefore from this perspective the screen was totally unusable, still needing marketing research."], "length": 10667, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Project Manager seems to be somewhat disappointed with the prototype, particularly with the design of the remote control, which they think is not practical and would be difficult to hold in one's hand. They also express frustration with the costs, which they feel are too high, and the fact that they had to compromise on the design to meet the budget. However, they do acknowledge that the team has done a good job with the design and that it is good-looking, but they are more concerned with the functionality and usability of the product."}
