{"input": "What did Grad F say about his proposal?", "answers": ["Grad F explained that he was focusing on writing his proposal for his qualification exams, which was on the 25th of July. He had to write a paper and pass it around before that date."], "length": 11886, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "Grad F mentioned that he had been working on his proposal and had finished a section on intermediate categories that he wants to classify. He plans to use a reasonable set of intermediate features, such as regular phonetic features, to start off and then build a system that classifies these features using multi-band techniques. He hopes to get a full rough draft done by Monday and then give it to Morgan."}
{"input": "What did the group discuss about speech recognition?", "answers": ["Since the marketing and the user interface designer mentioned speech recognition in their previous presentations, the industrial designer suggested putting a simple speech recogniser on the digital signal processing chip and training the speech recogniser for particular users. However, the user interface designer concerned that it would be too complex. Then the industrial designer argued that even some simple mobile devices could have these voice dialers so it wouldn't be so sophisticated, and the marketing also said the product would be good to sell with the speech recogniser."], "length": 7338, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The group discussed the possibility of incorporating speech recognition into the remote control, with the User Interface Designer suggesting that it could be a useful feature, especially for users who want to control the volume or change channels without having to press buttons. The Marketing Expert noted that speech recognition could be a selling point, especially for young people who are familiar with voice-controlled devices. However, the group also discussed the potential complexity and cost of implementing speech recognition, with the Industrial Designer suggesting that it could be done with a simple speech recogniser on the D_S_B_ chip. The group also considered the idea of training the speech recogniser for individual users, but ultimately decided that it might be too complex and expensive."}
{"input": "What were the two lessons that can be learned from early implementer areas?", "answers": ["Huw Irranca-Davies mentioned two things that their teams learned from early implementer areas. The first one was how to deal with bureaucracy and the second was about communication with both parents and pilot authorities."], "length": 12333, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The two lessons that can be learned from early implementer areas are: 1) the administrative burden of the current approach, which requires parents to provide documents such as wage slips and birth certificates, and 2) the challenge of communication, where parents outside of the pilot areas are asking why they can't access the offer, and parents within pilot areas are asking why they don't qualify for the offer despite meeting other eligibility criteria."}
{"input": "What did the User Interface think of using titanium when discussing product cost and quotation?", "answers": ["The User Interface thought it was reasonable to replace the titanium with titanium-coloured plastic. When discussing how to reduce the cost of the product, the user interface designer insisted that if they could only choose to save the titanium exterior or the LCD screen, it would be a better choice to save the LCD screen and dump the titanium cover but replacing it with hard plastics. The result was the major of the group including Marketing agreeed the suggestion."], "length": 10674, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The User Interface suggested replacing the titanium with titanium-coloured plastic to reduce costs, stating \"Well, if we uh we we have to get cheaper.\" This implies that they thought using titanium was a luxury that could be sacrificed to meet budget constraints."}
{"input": "Summarize the discussion about trend watching and appearance design.", "answers": ["The marketing put forward three noteworthy aspects in trends. First and foremost, people loved fancy things that they could be identified with. The second point was that as a remote control it had to be technologically innovative. Thirdly, being easy to use was also necessary. From a broader perspective, fruit and vegetables were in fashion this year and being spongy was also popular. Thus, contrary to the industrial designer, the marketing thought rubber was more feasible in terms of sponginess. The group agreed that the product should resemble fruit and vegetable in shape and colour but the specific design was not decided."], "length": 6957, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The discussion about trend watching and appearance design centered around the idea that remote controls should have a \"fancy\" and \"futuristic\" look, with a focus on being \"fancy\" and \"technologically innovative\" rather than being easy to use. The Marketing Expert presented a trend analysis, suggesting that remote controls should have a \"fruit and vegetable\" look, be spongy, and have a natural shape, with a focus on colors and materials that are currently in style. The Industrial Designer and User Interface Designer discussed the feasibility of incorporating these trends into the design, with the Industrial Designer suggesting that they could use rubber or other materials to achieve a spongy texture, and the User Interface Designer proposing that they could make the remote control smell like fruit. The team ultimately decided to explore two options: a spongy, fruity-like design and a titanium design that smells like fruit, with the goal of creating a design that is both trendy and functional."}
{"input": "What did the professor think about echoes and reverberation?", "answers": ["The professor thought it was possible to reduce the effects of reverberation by removing the low-energy segments. He thought a VAD-like approach would work. This would make it so that the model was more likely to keep an echo than throw out speech."], "length": 11887, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The professor thought that echoes and reverberation could be a problem in the re-synthesis of speech, and suggested that it might be possible to get rid of them by using a high-pass filtering approach, similar to what Hirsch did in the late 1980s. He also mentioned that the reverberation time in the room was likely around 0.7-0.8 seconds, and suggested that a measurement should be taken to confirm this. Additionally, he noted that the SRI system was putting in some reverberation, but also taking out some, and that this could be causing some issues."}
{"input": "What did the group talk about the appearance of the television remote?", "answers": ["In terms of putting fashion into the electronics, this is both their slogan and their design target. Apart from the company colour of black and red, the Project Manager came up with the idea of providing skins for the users. But the members were not in favour of this idea for that the work would be huge, at the same time, they were inspired to add the logo on the boot-up screen and introduce a dormant mode because most people do not want the remote to be flashing when the TV is on."], "length": 5336, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The group discussed the appearance of the television remote, with the Industrial Designer suggesting the use of high-grade recycled plastic for the casing and the Marketing team recommending a sleek and simple design. They also considered the idea of using a touch screen for the basic functions, with the Project Manager suggesting that the company colors and logo could be incorporated into the design, possibly as a temporary display on the boot-up screen. The group also discussed the possibility of using different skins or interfaces for the touch screen, but ultimately decided that this might be too complex and expensive."}
{"input": "What did the group think about Dave leaving?", "answers": ["The group needed Dave Johnson to install the some one hundred SUN - Blades that had arrived and would help with storage. But Dave Johnson, the professor informed them, was leaving for ten days. The Professor informed the group that Dave was always busy and the group realized that there was no dedicated person who could help them."], "length": 12802, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The group seemed to be aware that Dave was leaving for 11 days, and Professor B mentioned that he had finally reached his \"saturation point\" and was too busy to help with the project. The group also joked about Dave's absence, with Professor B saying \"I guess we reached it\" and Grad A responding with a laugh."}
{"input": "What did the group discuss about the trend of teacher recruiting and the reasons for it, and steps they had taken?", "answers": ["Although there were no exact figures,they had seen over recent years that they were only recruiting to about 65 percent of targets. It was partially due to the financial incentives, but that was not all of the problems. Because this issue was not only in Wales, but a common issue in all over the world. For example, in England, despite higher financial incentives to join ITE courses, people still were not getting onto those courses. To solve this problem, they had taken several steps including making sure their ITE provision was world class, embarking on the first national ITE recruitment marketing exercise, etc. In this process, Llyr Gruffydd had a doubt that the level of ambition did not reflect the level of action that was needed actually. Kirsty Williams responded that different statistics told different stories. They obviously understood there were many steps they had to take, but they were trying instead of just sitting back and waiting."], "length": 13883, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The group discussed the trend of teacher recruiting, specifically the difficulty in recruiting teachers to train through the medium of Welsh, and the reasons for it, which included a decline in the number of students choosing to study through the medium of Welsh after leaving school. They also discussed the steps they had taken to address this issue, including the introduction of a part-time PGCE route, financial incentives, and a national ITE recruitment marketing exercise. The Cabinet Secretary for Education, Kirsty Williams, stated that the issue was not unique to Wales and was a global problem, citing examples from the USA, Finland, and Australia. She also mentioned that the group had set up an advisory board to understand the issues preventing people from becoming teachers and to identify solutions."}
{"input": "What was said on speech overlap?", "answers": ["Efforts by speaker mn005 are in progress to detect overlapping speech. For a single transcribed meeting, speaker mn005 reported approximately 300 cases of overlap. Future work will involve manually deriving time marks from sections of overlapping speech for the same meeting, and then experimenting with different measures, e.g. energy increase, to determine a set of acoustically salient features for identifying speaker overlap. "], "length": 19653, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The discussion on speech overlap centered around PhD D's work on detecting overlapping speech events in a meeting recording. He explained that he was considering all acoustic events, including silence, and had found 1,000 acoustic events in 12 minutes, including 300 overlapping speech events. The group discussed how he was defining an overlapping event, with PhD D explaining that he considered a contiguous region of overlapping speech as a single event, regardless of the number of speakers involved. PhD C suggested that the group could use the relative energy in the close-talking mikes to infer speaker overlap, rather than manually marking it. PhD D mentioned that he had found a difference in the signal collected by the far-field mike compared to the mixed file, and that he was considering processing only the speech signal collected by the objective signal. The group also discussed the idea of using a combination of close-talking mikes to detect speaker overlap, and PhD D mentioned that he had found a low signal-to-noise ratio in the speech file collected by the far-field mike."}
{"input": "What was decided on the microphone procedures?", "answers": ["Following close-talking microphone recognition procedures, it was decided that data from the far-field microphones (or PZMs) should be tested on the recognizer as soon as possible. Speaker mn017 will compare close-talking microphone recognition results with those obtained for downsampled data. The SRI recognizer will be set up at ICSI to enable researchers to run their own variants."], "length": 12785, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The group discussed and decided on several microphone procedures, including the use of wireless microphones, the placement of microphones, and the handling of channel changes. They also discussed the possibility of using a hand-held microphone that can be passed around to different speakers, and decided to order a custom-made headset to replace the current Sony headsets. Additionally, they decided to use a cross-pad to allow speakers to indicate when they want to make a comment or request to be recorded."}
