{"input": "Summarize the discussion about project finance.", "answers": ["The production cost was 12.5 Euros. So, the group decided to have one simple battery, no kinetic energy, no solar, one advanced chip on print, one sample speaker, one single-curved surface which can be folded, push-buttons, the scroll wheel, the LCD display. And they decided to give up the sample sensor as well as the locator."], "length": 9368, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The discussion about project finance began with the Project Manager mentioning that they suspect they'll have a couple of minor finance issues, but they're sure they can get around them somehow. They then joked about sending manufacturing to a poor country to cut costs. The team discussed the production costs using an Excel spreadsheet, and they decided to remove the sample sensor and the locator to stay within their budget of €12.50. They also considered removing the scroll wheel, but ultimately decided to keep it and instead use two buttons on the side for up and down navigation. The team was able to bring the costs within budget, with only €1 left over for unforeseen expenses."}
{"input": "Why did Industrial Design agree with the Marketing when discussing their presentations on the appearance design?", "answers": ["While discussing their presentations on the appearance design, both the Industrial Design and the Marketing put forward to use rubber as the material rather than other things like wood. The Industrial Design agreed to choose rubber because it can stand beat, drop and extrusion."], "length": 7882, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "Industrial Designer agreed with Marketing's suggestion to use a rubber material for the remote control's case because it tends to be associated with being durable and can withstand being dropped, unlike other materials that may break or crack."}
{"input": "Why did Project Manager agree with Marketing when assessing the criteria of matching the operating behaviour of the user?", "answers": ["Industrial Designer mentioned that they were considering that the remote was more for right-handed people than for left-handed people. Project Manager said that there didn't always have all left-handers or all right-handers in a family. Marketing mentioned that users could use their finger to scroll rather than their thumb and this would negate the whole RSI issue. Project Manager agreed on it and the group would need an investigation."], "length": 9381, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "Project Manager agreed with Marketing that the remote control's design was suitable for the majority of users, who are right-handed, and therefore did not consider the issue of left-handed users to be a major problem."}
{"input": "Summarize the whole meeting.", "answers": ["It was the last meeting of the group and focused on detail design. First, the user interface designer displayed the appearance and the texture of the product with a working prototype. After that, the industrial designer gave some technical specifications on the product. In the product evaluation that included several criteria, the controller got an average score of two point one seven, which implied it was good. When it came to the quotation of product components, the original one was seventeen dot eight dollars, which was apparently over budget. After some discussions about battery and operations on the calculator, the group finally cut the cost down to under twelve dollars. Finally, they did some self-assessment and celebrated the completion of the project."], "length": 8554, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting was a project design meeting where the team presented and discussed a new remote control prototype, a strawberry-shaped device with various features such as speech recognition, location finding, and a hand dynamo to generate electricity. The team evaluated the prototype based on several criteria, including fanciness, technology, robustness, usefulness, size and weight, and adaptability, and gave it an average score of 2.17 out of 7. The team also discussed the financial aspects of the project, including the cost of the prototype and the need to stay within a budget of $12.50. The meeting concluded with a positive evaluation of the project and a decision to celebrate its success with a party."}
{"input": "What did Marketing talk about market trend findings?", "answers": ["There were two groups, the young and trendy, and the old and rich. For the first group, they would like fruit and vegetables as a theme for clothes, shoes, and also products. And for the material, a spongy feeling one would be better. But the old and rich would like dark colors, simple, and recognizable shapes as well as familiar material, wood especially."], "length": 6030, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "Marketing discussed market trend findings, specifically highlighting that young and trendy people are starting to like fruit and vegetables as a theme for clothes, shoes, and products, and prefer materials with a spongy feeling. On the other hand, older and richer people prefer dark colors, simple, recognizable shapes, and familiar materials, especially wood."}
{"input": "What was concluded on semantic specification?", "answers": ["The revised semantic specification and construction formalism are more stable than the previous versions. In the latter, we find both construction types and meaning types along with formal considerations like verb subcategorization, or the ones a \"directed motion\" construction would dictate."], "length": 24580, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The group discussed and refined their semantic specification notation, including the use of form-meaning pairs, designates, and relations. They also clarified the meaning of terms such as \"profile\", \"designate\", and \"focus\", and discussed how to represent information structure, including topic and focus. The notation was intended to be a tool kit for building the semantic part of constructions, and the group aimed to make it more precise and consistent."}
{"input": "Summarize the meeting", "answers": ["The meeting concerned mostly status updates and logistical affairs. The participants learned that the transcription status was good. The progress on the DARPA demo was also good, except the interface did not look as pretty as they had wanted to. The storage disks, which they had been waiting for, had finally arrived, but the person responsible for installing them was going on break. The participants also decided to schedule less meetings, so they have updates to report when they do. The meeting ended with technical discussion about model performance."], "length": 12797, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting discussed various topics, including the transcription status, participant approval, DARPA demo status, and disk issues. The team also discussed the Transcriber interface and potential improvements, such as adding a new window to display arbitrary valued functions. Additionally, they talked about the recognition system, including the latest results and plans for future improvements. The meeting also touched on the issue of disk space and the need to expand storage capacity. Finally, the team discussed the possibility of having a separate meeting for recognition-related topics and the idea of alternating the focus of the meeting between data collection and recognition."}
{"input": "Summarize the discussion about technical function design.", "answers": ["The user interface designer stressed that they should design nothing but a remote control because their principle was to make users' simple desires into simple actions. Therefore, the proposed concept for the design included just a few buttons, a screen with a back light which can change colours and titanium, which was accepted by the group."], "length": 7337, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The discussion about technical function design was led by Mark Dwight, the User Interface Designer, who presented a concept for a simple and universal remote control. He emphasized the importance of Occam's Razor, avoiding unnecessary complexity, and making the remote control easy to use. The team discussed various features, including automatic on/off, volume control, and speech recognition, with some members expressing concerns about the feasibility and cost of these features. The team also considered the possibility of gesture recognition, but ultimately decided against it due to its high cost. The discussion concluded with a focus on the component concept, with the team agreeing to work on individual components, including the user interface, trend watching, and component design."}
{"input": "What were the main discussion points of the meeting?", "answers": ["The group discussed topics including a potential collaboration with another ICSI member regarding the analysis of inference structures, efforts by speaker mn005 to detect speaker overlap, the current status of recordings and transcriptions, and future efforts to collect meeting data. In addition to weekly meetings by the BMR group, efforts are in progress to record meetings by other ICSI research groups, as well as routine discussions by non-ICSI members."], "length": 19656, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The main discussion points of the meeting included the analysis of a meeting transcript, specifically the detection of overlapping speech and the development of a system to automatically identify and mark speaker overlaps. The group also discussed the use of close-talking mikes versus far-field mikes, the importance of having a controlled environment for data collection, and the potential for collecting additional data from local broadcast media, such as radio shows. Additionally, the group touched on the topic of echo cancellation and the possibility of using Brian Kingsbury's work as a reference point. Other discussion points included the progress of the Meeting Recorder project, the collection of data, and the potential for using the data to study human interactions and behavior."}
{"input": "Summarize the discussion about hourly rates of the service.", "answers": ["In this part, the meeting talked about the affordability of £4.50 amount (the universal amount) and add-ups for some cases. The group separately discussed the situation of families of different incomes levels and different areas."], "length": 12329, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The discussion about hourly rates of the childcare service centered around the proposed £4.50 rate, which has been welcomed by the sector as a universal rate that is simpler than the complex offer in England. However, concerns were raised by some committee members, including Mark Reckless and Darren Millar, that the rate may not be sufficient in more expensive areas, such as Cardiff and Newport, and that it may lead to a situation where wealthier parents can afford to pay for additional services within the settings, while lower-income families may be priced out. The Minister, Huw Irranca-Davies, acknowledged these concerns and stated that the rate would be reviewed if necessary, but emphasized that the current rate has been welcomed by the sector and is seen as a key aspect of the scheme's simplicity and affordability. He also noted that the rate is above the average rate in England and that the sector is not currently arguing for an increase."}
{"input": "What was Kirsty Williams' opinion of the decision of not accrediting the University of South Wales with the ability to deliver teacher training?", "answers": ["Kirsty Williams thought this process was independent of the Government, and it would not be very appropriate to comment on it. From the very outset of their ITE reforms, they had made it clear that they expected very high standards in the ITE system, but this process was independent of the Government. More specifically with the concern of the impact on students and staff, Kirsty Williams mentioned that this would be a responsibility of University of South Wales itself."], "length": 13885, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "Kirsty Williams stated that she wouldn't comment on the decision as it was an independent process, but acknowledged that the University of South Wales had concerns about the impact of the decision on students and staff. She also confirmed that the Welsh Government would continue to commission training places from accredited institutions, and that the university would be responsible for ensuring that students already in the system could complete their studies."}
