{"input": "Why did the Industrial Designer disagree to replace the titanium when discussing product cost and quotation?", "answers": ["The Industrial Designer suggested that the titanium provided the tough look and the strong cover to protect the product. Besides, the older customers would like it much more because it fit their taste. So the titanium was important and shouldn't be replacing by titanium-like plastic or saving the LCD screen but dumping the titanium."], "length": 10675, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Industrial Designer disagreed to replace the titanium because he thought it was an important feature that provided a tough look and feel, and also because older people would like it due to its classical appearance. He believed that the titanium was a key aspect of the product's design and functionality, and that replacing it with a cheaper material would compromise the product's overall quality and appeal."}
{"input": "Summarize the ways of continuing mental health service during the lockdown.", "answers": ["Vaughan Gething pointed out that due to the school lockdown, children as well as their parents would face difficulties at home learning and social-contacting. In case they would suffer from potential mental health problems, the government have to carry out a mental health recovery plan. Sian Gwenllian then mentioned that the CAMHS, as well as all the schools, should follow up the mental states of children and offer telephone and online support."], "length": 10497, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "During the lockdown, the Welsh Government has continued to provide mental health services, including child and adolescent mental health services (CAMHS), through online and telephone support, with a focus on remote delivery and phone or online consultations where possible. The Government has also expanded online provision of services, including online support services and telephone advice services, and has encouraged people to use these services, with the aim of preventing a rise in other diseases and avoiding unintended consequences such as a rise in measles. Additionally, the Government has ensured that crisis care services, including those for children and young people, continue to be available, with seven-day-a-week crisis care services maintained."}
{"input": "Summarize the discussion about the design of remote control for children.", "answers": ["The group thought remote controls should keep children safe while using them. At the same time, the remote control should be brightly coloured. Even if it is not colourful, it should also choose a monochrome colour that children like to attract them."], "length": 3236, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The discussion about the design of a remote control for children focused on making it robust, waterproof, and baby-proof, with features such as a universal design that can be used by both right- and left-handed individuals, a simple and intuitive interface, and a small size that can fit in a child's hand. The team also discussed the importance of making it colorful and visually appealing, with the idea of changing colors to make it more engaging for children. Additionally, they considered incorporating features such as a \"go back\" button, a history function, and the ability to record and play back sequences of actions, but ultimately decided to keep the design simple and easy to use."}
{"input": "What was the solution to risk appetite?", "answers": ["Dr David Blaney believed that governing bodies were not likely to exhibit as an imprudent risk appetite since they would see the potential risks coming through in forecasts, in requests for borrowings, predominantly and many other ways. Although the government body included a slightly different perspective, divisions were not falling short. Besides, commitments from enthusiastic people and occasionally with a slightly external perspective would also be a help."], "length": 10199, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The solution to risk appetite was not explicitly stated as a specific action, but rather as a process of HEFCW's involvement in helping institutions to think again about their aspirations and asking the right questions, rather than simply second-guessing their decisions."}
