{"input": "What did the Project Manager think of the profit when discussing the financial issue and why?", "answers": ["According to the Project Manager, the finance department proposed to price the product at 25 euros and make a profit of fifty million euros in total. However, the cost might be at only twelve fifty, meaning that the profit would be at one hundred percent."], "length": 5989, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Project Manager thought that the profit of 50 million Euros on the remote control was \"pretty dear\" and that selling it for 25 Euros would be \"pretty expensive\", implying that he was skeptical about the financial viability of the project."}
{"input": "What did the user interface designer recommend to do when discussing technical function design?", "answers": ["The user interface designer recommended to have a screen with a backlight which could change colours and to put all the options into this screen so that few buttons were needed and users would find it easy to use. As for the material, titanium was preferred because it was unbreakable and baby-proof. The user interface designer also suggested making the power and volume control fully automatic."], "length": 7344, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The user interface designer, Mark Dwight, recommended to \"provide simple desires into simple actions\" and to \"be simple\" when designing the remote control, as a way to make it easy to use and appealing to the market. He also suggested using Occam's Razor, which states that the simplest solution is often the best one, to avoid complicating the design."}
{"input": "Summarize the whole meeting.", "answers": ["The meeting was about a preliminary idea of new remote control, covering the price, the functions, the appearance and the name. After a brief self-introduction, Project Manager assigned the task. One of the most important issues of the meeting was about the price. Project Manager supposed the product should be sold at 25 euros with a one hundred percent profit. As it would be a multifunctional remote control, the members were confident that it would stand alone. Moving on to the issue of the appearance, the group analyzed the problems of the existing remotes and briefly talked about the user interface as well as came up with a name of the product."], "length": 5977, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting was an introductory session for a project to design a universal remote control. The Project Manager, Marty, explained the project's goals and objectives, including making a 100% profit on the remote control, which is expected to be sold for 25 Euros. The team discussed the design requirements, including making it user-friendly, sleek, and fashionable, and possibly incorporating features such as a touch screen, multi-format capabilities, and a locator device. They also brainstormed ideas for the remote control's design, including using a material similar to Apple's surgical white or titanium, and discussed the importance of considering the target market and pricing strategy. The meeting concluded with the team agreeing to work on the project and Marty assigning tasks to each team member."}
{"input": "What's the conclusion of the discussion about intuitive when presenting on evaluation criteria?", "answers": ["The group agreed that the remote had a high score in terms of locatability, so they first thought it was also intuitive. Then, Industrial Designer mentioned that it might be a little more difficult for people to get used to in the beginning because the scroll was a new technology. Marketing thought it was obvious how to use it. Therefore, groupmates agreed to give an average score of five."], "length": 9375, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The team concludes that the remote control is not entirely intuitive, but rather \"intuitive but different\", with the standard layout for numbers being three three three and one, rather than the way it's presented. They decide to give it a score of five, acknowledging that it's not immediately intuitive, but still a good design."}
{"input": "How did Kirsty Williams respond to university mergers?", "answers": ["Kirsty Williams said he was in favor of a healthy, strong and sustainable HE sector. If individual institutions wish to collaborate, that was a matter for themselves. They did not pursue or urge a policy of mergers, but if individual institutions felt that was of benefit to them and their students, they would have an interest in that and make sure that they were robust plans."], "length": 13870, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "Kirsty Williams stated that she is \"agnostic\" on the issue of university mergers, but emphasized that her priority is a healthy, strong, and sustainable higher education sector. She noted that individual institutions are autonomous and can choose to collaborate or merge if they see fit, and that the Welsh Government's role is to ensure that any mergers are robust and in the best interests of students."}
