{"input": "What did Industrial Designer say about the customers' taste?", "answers": ["Industrial Designer said that the younger people fancied soft materials and primary colors. For example, total yellow or totally red could be visible. The shapes could be curved and round,which would be easier to use. It would be better to use the basic battery so everybody could get it at the supermarket, or to use a kinetic battery after shaking it a few times, it could be loaded. Industrial Designer suggested hard plastic material and rubber since the younger people liked that. The survey also discovered that the young favored a scroll push like the mouse to use for channels and volume."], "length": 6031, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Industrial Designer mentioned that younger people want a remote control with soft materials, primary colors, curved and round shapes, and a shape that fits in the hand, making it easier to use. They also want a remote control that is different from the traditional straight and flat ones."}
{"input": "Why did the Marketing think they should have LCD when presenting the market trends?", "answers": ["When Industrial Designer mentioned that LCD was basically feedback from television and questioned the necessity of LCD, Marketing suggested that they could integrate with the television then it could come up with new information about what was on, and users could just see that on the remote rather than TV itself so that users won't get interrupted by viewing pleasure. Also, Marketing thought the group could have a minimalist remote control so that users could flip it open if they wanted to mess about it."], "length": 8984, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Marketing Expert thought they should have LCD because it was a key feature that users wanted in the near future, and it was considered technologically innovative and different, which was important for the target group. They also believed that it would make the remote control look fancy and have a high-tech feel, which was a key aspect of the market trends."}
{"input": "What was the future of data collection?", "answers": ["The group aims to collect over 100 hours of Meeting Recorder data in total. Speaker consent forms are being revised. It was suggested that subjects should sign a new consent form after 10 recording sessions."], "length": 19654, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The future of data collection was expected to continue at a rate of 3-4 meetings per week, with a goal of collecting 50-60 hours of data, which would include a mix of meeting recordings and other types of data, such as radio shows and television programs. The team was also considering collecting data from other sources, including local broadcast media, and was open to the idea of recording additional meetings, such as lunch meetings, to supplement the existing data."}
{"input": "What did Julie Morgan elaborate on their work with the foster organizations when talking about the adequate social support for those children who were in foster?", "answers": ["According to Julie Morgan, the group had been in close cooperation with the fostering organizations in Welsh to guarantee the children in need. To people's relief, the fostering organizations were running well with enough staff and equipment during the pandemic so that the children could enjoy good care there. What's more, the fostering organizations prolonged their helpline time in case some emergencies would happen. Other support measures, say, the fund for the foster carers, would be followed up."], "length": 10512, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "Julie Morgan AM elaborated on the Welsh Government's work with foster organizations, stating that they have had close communications with them and have supported the Association of Fostering and Adoption Cymru (AFA Cymru) to develop guidance for foster carers, which has been welcomed by the sector. She also mentioned that they are working with the third sector, including the Fostering Network and Voices from Care, to provide support for foster carers and care leavers, and that these organizations have developed specific offers to support care leavers and foster carers during the pandemic."}
{"input": "What did D think about the experimental setup of the computer navigation system?", "answers": ["D wanted to know if the videos for the navigation system would be recorded from a first person perspective. D was also trying to understand how ambitious the system was. D learned that the goal was to understand how people reacted and talked in such a scenario. D provided some information about potential candidates for the human operator and wizard roles as well."], "length": 15412, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "D thought that the experimental setup was a good starting point, but had some concerns about the simplicity of the tasks and the lack of contextual factors that could influence the interpretation of the user's requests. They also wondered if the data collection was suitable for studying intention, given that the tasks were not differentiated by intentionality and there was no label for the user's intention."}
{"input": "Summarize the whole meeting", "answers": ["The meeting was about a computer based navigation system that will be used to conduct linguistic experiments on navigational terminology and intentionality, and it was simultaneously being recorded for another project. The navigational interface allows people to walk around a place and perform basic actions, like entering a building. Its goal is to understand what people intend to do based on granular linguistic features. The experimental set up is that people talk to a human assistant who controls the program in an attempt to complete a certain task. To study intentionality, the team will determine important linguistic features and use a Bayes model to see if they can predict the intention based on utterances."], "length": 15403, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting was a discussion among researchers and students about a project to develop a system that can infer the intentions of users based on their language and context. The system, called Meeting Recorder, aims to record and analyze meetings to understand how people communicate and interact with each other. The team discussed various aspects of the project, including the data collection process, the use of a belief net to infer intentions, and the development of a prototype system. They also talked about the challenges of extracting features from the data and how to make the system more robust and accurate. Additionally, they discussed the use of XML and Java packages to develop the system and the potential for using corpora from other projects to improve the system's performance. The meeting concluded with a plan to have the students work on developing a belief net package and to have a follow-up meeting to discuss their progress."}
{"input": "What was the discussion about the government's policy to improve long-term care facilities and protect the seniors?", "answers": ["The opposition party claimed that people across Canada were appalled by the situation in care homes which left seniors suffering. The minister explained that the government was fully aware of the situation. The minister promised that they would fully play their role federal level with advice, with guidance, with support and with investments. They were going to have those conversations about how best they can improve the care for all seniors."], "length": 20279, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The discussion centered around the government's response to the COVID-19 pandemic in long-term care facilities, with several members expressing outrage and concern over the horrific conditions and neglect reported by the Canadian Armed Forces. The Minister of Health, Patty Hajdu, acknowledged the severity of the situation and committed to working with provinces and territories to improve care and ensure that seniors are protected. However, the NDP and other opposition members pressed for more concrete action, including binding national standards, a public inquiry, and the removal of profit from long-term care facilities. The Minister of Health ultimately committed to working with provinces and territories to find a solution, but did not provide a clear timeline or specific actions to address the crisis."}
{"input": "What did Albert Heaney think of the coronavirus Act when discussing the evaluations on the legitimacy of the children's rights, protection and demands?", "answers": ["When it comes to the evaluations on the legitimacy of the children's rights, protection and demands, Nicola Edwards mentioned that they had been working on the coronavirus Act to see if they could ease some of the statutory requirements, further helping the childcare and education affairs in the future. Then Albert Heaney went on to elaborate that it was because that they were not meant to include any easements in children's social services at first, however, it was obvious at present that at least for those carers, they had to break the law of social distance to keep looking after the children in need. Therefore, they were calling for the possibility of losing the law for those who did have something to do with childcare and education."], "length": 10509, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "Albert Heaney mentioned that the coronavirus Act was UK Government legislation, and therefore, the Welsh Government did not introduce any easements in relation to children's services legislation, which meant that children's rights impact assessments were not necessary."}
{"input": "How does the product manager want to achieve the expected profit when discussing the group's plan of their marketing strategy?", "answers": ["The project manager wants to sell the remote internationally for 25 euros and keep its cost under 12.50 euros to meet the global profit target of 50 million euros. But project managers don't calculate exactly how much they want to sell."], "length": 6397, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The product manager wants to achieve the expected profit of 50 million Euros by selling the new remote control at a price of 25 Euros internationally, while keeping the costs at 12.5 Euros, and by making the product original, trendy, and modern, with a design that is both familiar and international."}
{"input": "What did Vaughan Gething elaborate on the crisis care when talking about the evaluations on the increasing suicide crimes during the COVID-19 and the long-term special carefor the pregnant women?", "answers": ["When talking about the evaluations on the increasing suicide crimes during the COVID-19, Vaughan Gething said that in order to prevent more tragedies, they had been offering mental health care for the people. Even if most public service had to be shut down during the outbreak, the staff at the crisis help continued to be available. In addition, they had a seven-day-a-week crisis care, which they hoped that could also be a long-term service to help people in need even after this particular period."], "length": 10516, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "Vaughan Gething elaborated that the Welsh Government is already investigating the potential effects of the COVID-19 restrictions on the mental health and well-being of children and young people, including the risk of an increase in suicide, and has commissioned a review to be carried out by the delivery unit and the national advisory group. He also mentioned that the Government is committed to providing consistent perinatal support for all women across Wales, and that the perinatal mental health service has not been stopped, but has been adapted to be provided on a phone or online basis where possible."}
{"input": "Summarize the discussion about user interface concept.", "answers": ["The user interface designer presented a good-looking design with few buttons. As for the colour, the user interface designer suggested making a backlight of the LCD screen with different colours, but this was denied due to the high cost. Then the industrial designer recommended changing the colour of the assembly at an extra charge to meet special needs. Light and infrared were also taken into consideration to make the controller glow in the dark. In order to make a smart controller, the group mentioned speech recognition and user identification."], "length": 6955, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The discussion about the user interface concept began with User Interface presenting a concept called the \"Millennium Remote Control\" with a large number of buttons, but it was met with criticism for being too complex and ugly. The team then decided to simplify the design to a few buttons, with a low-profile look and a simple LCD display. User Interface suggested that the remote control could be made to change color, either by using a color LCD or by using a material that changes color depending on its surroundings. The team also discussed the idea of making the remote control \"smart\" by incorporating features such as speech recognition and fingerprint recognition, but ultimately decided to focus on making it easy to use and intuitive."}
{"input": "What was the finding of the market?", "answers": ["Marketing did a usability lab-test with a hundred people and found that users disliked the ugly look of current remote controls and the market segmentation includes different attitudes towards new functions. Users felt it hard to learn new functions and they zapped a lot. The channel switch was the most popular function while the power, volume and channel selections buttons were very relevant. The market was to be segmented to different groups and most users were between thirty-six and forty-five years old and young users had different tastes about the LCD compared to the old. The speech recognition features were welcomed by the second largest group, the young but which group they should targeted remained to be discussed. Marketing concluded that less important functions should be discarded and the device should be user friendly."], "length": 4754, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The market findings revealed that users dislike the look and feel of current remote controls, finding them ugly and hard to use. They also found that many remote controls have too many unused buttons, with only 10% of users using 50% of the buttons. The most used button is the channel selection, followed by power and volume buttons. The market also showed that younger users (16-25 years old) are more interested in new features such as LCD screens and speech recognition, but are also more critical of spending money. Older users (46-65 years old) are less interested in new features but are more likely to spend money."}
{"input": "What was the questioning about the reports on long-term care facilities?", "answers": ["The opposition party questioned that the report was delayed in issuing by the government and was not acted upon. The minister answered that the report was done and given up through the chain of command, and once the government received this report, it was forwarded to the appropriate authorities. The minister denied the fact that the report was released and acted upon late."], "length": 20273, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The questioning revolved around the reports from the Canadian Armed Forces detailing the horrific conditions in long-term care facilities, including neglect, abuse, and neglect of seniors, and the government's response to these findings, with opposition members calling for a full public inquiry, national standards for long-term care, and an end to profit in the care of seniors."}
{"input": "Summarize their discussions on university mergers.", "answers": ["Kirsty Williams firstly proposed that the main goal he would like to pursue was a healthy, strong and sustainable HE sector. They did not pursue or urge a policy of mergers, but they welcomed the mergers, if this was beneficial for individual institutions. Hefin David proposed that was in contrast with the predecessors, and talked about the situation when Cardiff Metropolitan University was under pressure from the previous Minister to merge with the University of Glamorgan and Newport. Kirsty Williams thought they needed to look at the future instead of looking at the past. They had a strong institution in Cardiff Met now. However, it was obvious that they got to learn some lessons from that process."], "length": 13868, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The committee discussed university mergers, with Hefin David AM expressing concerns that the University of South Wales, which resulted from a merger, had seen a reduction in activity in the Newport campus and that the merger had not been value for money. Cabinet Secretary Kirsty Williams AM stated that the Welsh Government does not have a policy of pursuing mergers, but rather supports individual institutions in their decisions to collaborate or merge. She also acknowledged that there were concerns about the merger and that HEFCW was conducting a review of the experience of reconfiguration to inform future policy."}
{"input": "What was the debate about a particular fundraising campaign?", "answers": ["The opposition party claimed that the Minister of Digital Government has been promoting a fundraising campaign to sue Global News for their story criticizing the Chinese Communist Party, and the opposition party thought the minister was supporting the Communist Party of China and threatening our media and freedom of expression. The Minister explained that community outreach was a very important part of the work of a member of Parliament and WeChat was one of many social media sites regularly used by members, and the minister did not share any personal views on Wechat."], "length": 20271, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The debate was about a fundraising campaign to sue Global News for their story criticizing the Chinese Communist Party, which was promoted by the Minister of Digital Government, Joyce Murray, and was seen as an attempt to muzzle a Canadian journalist and deprive Canadians of the facts about China."}
{"input": "Summarize the problem of the priority for the vulnerable children and adequate social support for those who were in foster.", "answers": ["The group mentioned that the vulnerable children and adequate social support should be carried out through the outbreak of the pandemic to protect them from infection or other mental and physical problems. The situation seemed awkward. However, fortunately, the group found that there were still enough staff available to offer mental support to those children. In terms of the children in foster, they thought the key problem at present was to ensure their living environment in the foster homes."], "length": 10506, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Welsh Government is aware that some vulnerable children and those in foster care may be falling through the cracks due to the COVID-19 pandemic, and there are concerns about the adequacy of social support for these groups. The Deputy Minister, Julie Morgan, acknowledged that some children's services are struggling to maintain contact with vulnerable children and families, and there has been a drop in safeguarding referrals to social services. However, she also noted that local authorities are working hard to maintain contact with vulnerable children and families, and that some children's residential care settings are functioning well despite the challenges posed by the pandemic. The Welsh Government is also working with the third sector to provide support for foster carers and care leavers, and has established a task and finish group to look at the issues affecting the care system."}
{"input": "Summarize the discussion about the functional design of the remote control.", "answers": ["The group felt that the remote control didn't need a lot of buttons,and on the basis of ensuring originality, it should also ensure conventionality and make it applicable to all kinds of groups."], "length": 3974, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The discussion about the functional design of the remote control revolved around identifying the necessary functions and components of the product. The Industrial Designer, Gaurav, asked for clarification on the difference between functional design and conceptual design, and the Project Manager explained that functional design involves identifying the specific functions and components of the remote control, while conceptual design is a broader approach that considers how people will use the product. The team also discussed the importance of making the remote control user-friendly and intuitive, and the Industrial Designer suggested that they aim for a design that is easy to use and has a high battery life."}
{"input": "Why did the group decide to give two points on usefulness when doing product evaluation?", "answers": ["The basic mode of the product had only two directional buttons with which people could control the volume and change channels. Except for the two main functions, other functions were on the LCD screen and there was a jog dial controlling the cursor on the LCD screen. Therefore, the controller was very easy to use."], "length": 8565, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The group decided to give two points on usefulness because they felt that the product's usefulness was somewhat limited, particularly in terms of its ability to perform certain functions, such as directly accessing channel 25. They noted that the user had to press two buttons and then select 25, which was considered less convenient than other remote controls."}
{"input": "Summarize the whole meeting.", "answers": ["The meeting was about caring Welsh children during the outbreak of COVID-19. Due to the coronavirus, the state was locked down and a strict social distance was required, and then most schools in Welsh were shut down. The group was concerned whether the existing support was enough to ensure their children get through such a period healthily, in terms of both mentally and physically. They listed several possible solutions, including having the vaccination on the susceptible group, offering psychological support for the children as well as the pregnant women, and raising a contingency fund. Through their asking and answering with the ministers, the group finally agreed on a bright blueprint for the education and economic recovery in Welsh."], "length": 10490, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting of the Children, Young People and Education Committee was held to discuss the impact of the coronavirus pandemic on children and young people in Wales. The Minister for Health and Social Services, Vaughan Gething AM, and the Deputy Minister for Health and Social Services, Julie Morgan AM, answered questions from committee members on various topics, including the impact of the pandemic on children's mental health, the availability of childcare services, the provision of technology for vulnerable children, and the response of the Welsh Government to the pandemic. The committee also discussed the challenges faced by the childcare sector, the need for a bespoke support package for providers, and the effectiveness of the provision of childcare services for key workers. The meeting concluded with a private session to discuss further matters."}
{"input": "What were the changes decided on the equipment?", "answers": ["The group decided to purchase one additional head-mounted crown microphone. A tentative decision was also made to acquire a hand-held wireless microphone to pass around to additional meeting participants should the installation of more close-talking microphones prove too difficult."], "length": 12786, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The group decided to order one more wireless microphone unit, which costs $350, and a receiver unit, which costs $430, for a total of $780. They also discussed the possibility of using a different headset with the wireless microphone, and decided to order one to test it out before purchasing more. Additionally, they considered purchasing a custom-made headset with a pigtail, but decided to wait and see if the new headset works well before investing in more."}
{"input": "Summarize their discussion on the decision of not accrediting the University of South Wales with the ability to deliver teacher training.", "answers": ["Kirsty Williams firstly made it clear that it would not be appropriate to comment this since this process was independent of Government. Hefin David then proposed the impact on students and staff. Kirsty Williams thought there would be a responsibility upon the University of South Wales to ensure that those students already in the system were able to complete their studies with the appropriate level of support and tuition to enable them to achieve their career aspirations and graduate from that program. The same was true for staff. Kirsty Williams thought that would be a matter for the institution. After this, they had some discussions on the geographic spread of provision."], "length": 13883, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Cabinet Secretary for Education, Kirsty Williams, stated that the decision to not accredit the University of South Wales with the ability to deliver teacher training was an independent process and it would not be appropriate for her to comment on it. However, she acknowledged that the decision would have an impact on students and staff, and that the university would need to ensure that students already in the system could complete their studies. She also confirmed that the Welsh Government would commission training places from accredited institutions, and that the university would need to provide support and tuition to enable students to complete their courses."}
{"input": "Summarize the whole meeting.", "answers": ["Firstly, User Interface suggested trendy and one-color cases, good icons on the big buttons since they would be recognizable and excite the young people. Secondly, Industrial Designer introduced that young people favored soft materials and primary colors, but trendy design on the button and the use of LCD display would increase cost since the budget had always been a big problem. Moreover, the team would aim at the younger market based on the decision of the former meeting and agreed that to make the device more profitable they should sell the product for longer than one year. The team also discussed the button function of the remote control but they had not come to an agreement. Lastly, Project Manager decided on a normal battery and no kinetic to control the budget."], "length": 6026, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting was a discussion among a project team consisting of a Project Manager, an Industrial Designer, a User Interface expert, and a Marketing representative to design a new remote control for a television. The team discussed various aspects of the design, including the user interface, components, and trends in the market. They decided to create two concepts: one with a Liquid Crystal Display (L_C_D_) and one without, and to use a normal battery as the cheapest option. The team also discussed the use of a joystick or scroll buttons, and decided to keep the scroll buttons for the younger target audience. The meeting concluded with the team agreeing to have the design ready for the next meeting, called the \"detailed design\" meeting, where they will finalize the design and prepare for production."}
{"input": "What did Grad F think about intermediate categories?", "answers": ["Grad F informed the team that he was building a system that classified intermediate categories with multi-band techniques. Then, to reduce error for phoneme recognition, the intermediate categories could be added to improve performance. The method could be replicated for large vocabulary tasks like switchboard."], "length": 11886, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "Grad F thought that intermediate categories were a \"perplexing problem\" and initially wanted to come up with a complete set of them to classify right away. However, he later decided to start with a reasonable set, such as regular phonetic features, and use multi-band techniques to classify these intermediate categories, and then iteratively add or remove categories based on the errors produced in phoneme recognition."}
{"input": "What did the group talk about the evaluations on the increasing suicide crimes during the COVID-19 and the long-term special care for the pregnant women?", "answers": ["Lynne Neagle pointed out that due to the outbreak of the pandemic, more people reported depression and committed suicide. To deal with the harsh problem, they might have to carry out a review over the accidental mortality through the period. At the same time, Vaughan Gething suggested that even if most public services were shut down, the mental care, especially the perinatal mental health service, could not be stopped, just in order to support the vulnerable people."], "length": 10511, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The group discussed the Welsh Government's investigation into the potential increase in suicide among children and young people during the COVID-19 pandemic, with the Minister for Health and Social Services, Vaughan Gething AM, stating that the Government has commissioned a review to be carried out by the delivery unit and the national advisory group, including Dr Ann John, to review the current unexpected deaths during the start of the pandemic in Wales. The review aims to understand the potential effects of the restrictions on the mental health and well-being of children and young people, and whether it is leading to a spike in suicide. The Minister also mentioned that the group will have a report on the review before the end of the month, and that the Welsh Government will write to the committee once they have received the report and looked at it.\n\nRegarding perinatal mental health, the group discussed the Welsh Government's continued provision of perinatal mental health services, including online and phone-based support, and the Minister's commitment to creating in-patient capacity for perinatal mental health services, despite the challenges posed by the pandemic. The Minister also acknowledged that there has been a reduction in the number of women seeking mother and baby unit provision, but emphasized that this is due to fear and lockdown, rather than a lack of need."}
{"input": "Summarize the whole meeting.", "answers": ["The group mainly talked about the extra deciding of the product at this meeting including the presentation of the prototype assessment, the discussion of the requirements and trends of marketing and the product cost and quotation. Firstly, the Industrial Designer introduced the prototype of the product. It had not only the round basic shape which was made of hard plastics and titanium using different colors, but also the buttons like channel and volumn. Later, the group discussed some details and changes of redesigning the logo, buttons and screens. Next, the Marketing Expert mentioned the exterior of the product, the material attraction, and how easy it was to learn or use the basic functions of the product. Besides, the group discussed some details of the cost of components and made the product cheaper by replacing the titanium by hard plastics with similar color. They finally got an estimate of fourteen point one Euros, which was above the budget. In the end, they discussed the distinction between the product and other devices, and celebrated the completion of the project."], "length": 10663, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting was a discussion among the project team, including the Project Manager, Industrial Designer, User Interface, and Marketing, to review the design of a new remote control device. They presented their design, which included a titanium back, a LCD screen, and a voice recorder, and discussed its features and functionality. The team also evaluated the device's design using a set of criteria, including its good looks, ease of use, and features, and discussed potential issues with the design, such as the thickness of the device and the placement of the LCD screen. Additionally, they discussed the costs of the device and the need to make it more affordable, and considered alternative materials, such as plastic, to reduce costs. The team also discussed the SMARTboard and digital pen, which were used during the meeting, and their usefulness and limitations. The meeting concluded with a discussion of the project's progress and the need to finalize the design and prepare for production."}
{"input": "Summarize the discussion about industrial components.", "answers": ["The industrial designer provided several options respectively for energy, material and interface. Among these options, the industrial designer preferred traditional battery, titanium and simple push buttons so that they would have enough money for speech recognition. After that, the user interface designer proposed an LCD display only for output, which might be taken into account. The group also talked about how to enable the users to find their controllers."], "length": 6954, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The discussion about industrial components focused on the design of the remote control's energy source, material, and interface. The team decided to use a simple battery instead of solar cells due to energy limitations, and opted for titanium as the primary material due to its durability and aesthetic appeal. They also discussed the use of push buttons instead of liquid crystal display (LCD) buttons to reduce costs and complexity, and considered incorporating speech recognition technology to enhance user experience. Additionally, they touched on the idea of using a rubber or spongy material for the remote control's body, but ultimately decided to explore this option further."}
{"input": "What did the Industrial Designer recommend to do when discussing the naming of remote control and why?", "answers": ["After coming up with the idea of multi plates, the Industrial Designer asked to give the remote control a name and patent it. Within a brief discussion, they named it as Leopard Print out of the hope that it would also be helpful in cold winter days."], "length": 5990, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Industrial Designer suggested patenting the name of the remote control, specifically the face plate, and also recommended coming up with a fuzzy or unique name, such as \"Leopard print\", to make it stand out."}
{"input": "What did the team think about the new idea of Industrial Designer concerning the cover of the device?", "answers": ["Industrial Designer suggested having a trendy remote control with bright color, red for instance. But Marketing argued that it might add to cost and make it more difficult to design if the cover were to be changed. Industrial Designer also suggested that young people could draw on the cover to make their own remote control more unique. But again, it was questioned by User Interface who were not sure about whether it would be profitable."], "length": 4765, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The team had mixed opinions about the new idea of Industrial Designer concerning the cover of the device, with some members finding it original and interesting, while others were skeptical about its feasibility and potential cost. Marketing and User Interface Designer expressed concerns that the cost of producing interchangeable covers might be too high, and that it might not be a good idea to pursue this concept. However, Industrial Designer argued that it could be a good way to make the device more trendy and appealing to customers, and that it could be done at a relatively low cost. Ultimately, the team decided not to pursue this idea, with the Project Manager stating that it was not necessary."}
{"input": "What did Project Manager think of the manual design when discussing the ease of use?", "answers": ["Project Manager thought the beeping device was not easy to learn but Industrial Marketing thought the design expert should put more time on the ease of use design than a manual because they didn't have much time. While Project Manager mentioned that they were a design team, they could find some writers to make a manual point which could help maintain consistency with the older remote manual for the users."], "length": 11310, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "Project Manager thought that people don't read manuals, and therefore, the design of the remote control should prioritize ease of use and intuitive functionality, rather than relying on a manual for instructions."}
{"input": "Why did User Interface think they should use more buttons than the current one when discussing the technical function design?", "answers": ["Compared with the current manufacturer design, User Interface mentioned that they should reduce the option to control the DVD. And according to the new requirements, they need to control the maximum number of buttons within ten."], "length": 11315, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "User Interface thought they should use more buttons than the current one because they wanted to include more functions, but they had to reject that idea due to the project requirements."}
{"input": "Summarize the presentations on working design, technical functions, functional requirements.", "answers": ["Firstly, Industrial Designer presented on working design, identifying a few basic components of the remote. From the functional design perspective, Industrial Designer introduced what exactly the components were and how they worked with each other. Next, User Interface presented on technical functions design and personally suggested a small size of remote, red in colour and easy to use with must-have buttons. Then, Marketing presented on functional requirements and suggested the group aiming at both the functionality and beauty of the remote."], "length": 8486, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Industrial Designer presented on the working design of the remote control, explaining the functional components and how they work together. The User Interface presented on technical functions design, discussing the method they used to identify the main functions of the remote control, which included sending messages to the television set, switching on and off, changing channels, and adjusting volume. The Marketing representative presented on functional requirements, discussing the results of a survey on remote control usability, including the importance of channel selection, volume selection, and power, as well as the need for a sleek and stylish design, long battery life, and voice recognition."}
{"input": "Summarize their evaluations on the legitimacy of the children's rights, protection and demands.", "answers": ["In terms of their evaluations on the legitimacy of the children's rights, protection and demands, it was said that the group was launching a survey of children to see what the children were thinking over the outbreak of the COVID-19. The survey was carried online, asking mainly about whether the students were willing to go back to school and how they view other related problems. However, there were still indeed some drawbacks of their evaluations over the coronavirus Act. For example, the group thought that they should go further and view the laws from a future perspective, and assess each case individually more closely."], "length": 10499, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Welsh Government acknowledged the potential impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on children's rights and well-being, with the Minister for Health and Social Services, Vaughan Gething AM, expressing concerns about the potential long-term effects on an entire generation of children. The Deputy Minister, Julie Morgan AM, stated that the Welsh Government is committed to protecting children's rights and has taken steps to ensure that vulnerable children are not left behind, including providing support for childcare settings and ensuring that children have access to technology to maintain contact with social workers. However, the committee also raised concerns about the potential for children's rights to be compromised, particularly in relation to safeguarding and child protection, and the need for the Welsh Government to take a more proactive approach to addressing these issues. The Minister and Deputy Minister acknowledged the challenges posed by the pandemic and the need for a coordinated response to protect children's rights and well-being."}
{"input": "Summarize the discussion on storage disks and computer connections", "answers": ["The participants learned that the storage disks had eventually arrived. They were waiting on the disks to be installed. Grad E volunteered storage space on their own computer for the meantime. The meetings were around 1.5 gigs uncompressed and .5 gigs compressed. So, the group needed substantial space. The professor encouraged the team members to talk to Dave, the person responsible for managing the organization's storage systems, and figure things out."], "length": 12803, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The discussion on storage disks and computer connections revolved around the need for additional disk space to store meeting data, with the current storage capacity running low. It was suggested that the team could use the newly acquired SUN-Blades to add scratch disks, and Professor B mentioned that the sysadmins prefer to have one external drive per machine. The team also discussed the possibility of storing data on the machine room's back room, but it was noted that it's not connected to the ICSI network and would require a wire to be run. Professor B suggested that the team should have a conversation with Dave Johnson to discuss the logistics of adding more disk space and connections."}
{"input": "What did Industrial Designer say about the structure of the device?", "answers": ["There were a lot of small wires connecting the components, the battery and the switches. On the top of the remote control, there was the chip, buttons with numbers and teletext. Industrial Designer suggested that the remote control should be small and contain only one long-lasting battery to cut cost. And a bleep could be added to remind the battery usage."], "length": 4758, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Industrial Designer mentioned that the remote control has a chip on top, which receives signals from the buttons and sends them to the infrared, which then sends them to the television. He also explained that when a button is pressed, it sends a signal to the chip, which then sends it to the sub-components, and finally to the infrared. Additionally, he mentioned that the remote control has a battery, which is connected to the chip and the buttons, and that the battery is sent to the chip when a button is pressed."}
{"input": "What did the Professor think about the results?", "answers": ["The professor noted that the results were better than he expected and that the MSG did nothing. He also noted that adding French and Spanish to the multilingual English model hurt the model."], "length": 14016, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Professor thought that the results were promising, but also noted that they were not yet conclusive. He mentioned that some of the experiments showed good results, but others did not, and that there were still many open questions and issues to be addressed. He also expressed some skepticism about the MSG approach, suggesting that it may be complicated and that it may be better to focus on simpler solutions in the short term. However, he also acknowledged that the results were interesting and worth exploring further."}
{"input": "Summarize the meeting", "answers": ["The meeting began with the team catching up the professor, who had missed the previous meeting. Then, the team moved onto talking about echo cancelling techniques. They were trying to get the voice of other people from the primary person's microphone. Then, the team discussed how they could improve the performance on the Aurora task by playing around with the model. The team ended the meeting with various comments on different topics."], "length": 8183, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting discussed various topics related to speech recognition and audio processing, including the downsampling problem, LDA filters, and echo cancellation. The group reviewed the meeting with Hynek, which they had missed, and discussed the progress made on the research projects, including the use of Ericsson's approach for noise removal and the development of a new front-end for the Aurora task. They also discussed the use of acoustic features, such as the sonorant detector, and the importance of robustness in speech recognition systems. Additionally, the group discussed the use of Matlab and Octave for batch processing and the need for a standard in the field."}
{"input": "Summarize the whole meeting.", "answers": ["This meeting was about the detailed design. Firstly, User Interface presented on the prototype, including the shape, the size of the remote and the user interface including the buttons and the scrolling wheel. Next, the group had an evaluation criterion on the prototype. They scored an overall six point five average on the prototype and thought they had done well on it. Then, the group had a discussion about project finance. The group decided not to have a sample sensor and the locator because they had a production cost of 12.5 Euros. Lastly, the group had a discussion about the project process, including the use of a pen, the whiteboard and the PowerPoint. Also, the group was satisfied with Project Manager's leadership and Project Manager also mentioned that their costs were within the budget as well as some admin work."], "length": 9366, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting was a detailed design meeting for a new product, a remote control, where the team presented their prototype and discussed its features, design, and functionality. The team consisted of an Industrial Designer, a User Interface expert, a Marketing expert, and a Project Manager. They evaluated the product against a set of criteria, including its attractiveness, usability, and technological innovation, and assigned scores to each criterion. The team also discussed the production costs and made adjustments to the design to fit within their budget. The meeting was productive, and the team was able to complete their tasks and provide feedback on the project. The Project Manager concluded the meeting, thanking the team for their hard work and efficiency."}
{"input": "Did Kirsty Williams feel there had been any disadvantages to Cardiff Met because that merger did not go ahead?", "answers": ["Kirsty Williams was not aware of any disadvantages to Cardiff Met. In terms of students and staff, there would be some personal impact on them. However, in terms of institutions going forward, Kirsty Williams was not aware that they were currently struggling with any disadvantages. Instead of looking at the past, Kirsty Williams thought they needed to look at the future. It had turned out that they got a strong institution in Cardiff Met."], "length": 13881, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "Kirsty Williams stated that she was not personally aware of any disadvantages to Cardiff Met as a result of the merger not going ahead, but acknowledged that staff and students who were caught up in the deliberations and issues surrounding the merger may have had a personal impact."}
{"input": "Summarize the whole meeting.", "answers": ["This meeting was about the functional design of the remote control. Firstly, the Project Manager mentioned new requirements including one recognizable button in the middle, the handy design, rubber materials and they would not use teletext. Next, Marketing presented on functional requirements including a fancy look, ease of use, and the LCD as well as speech recognition. Then, Industrial Designer introduced technical design and working design on remote interfaces. Lastly, User Interface presented on component design and the group decided to adopt a user-centered approach to reduce the main buttons and put a big circle in the middle with the power button, channel, and volume buttons."], "length": 11299, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting was a discussion among a team of designers and a project manager to design a new remote control for a television. They discussed various features and requirements, including the use of an LCD screen, speech recognition, and a user-friendly interface. The team also debated the placement of the LCD screen, with some members suggesting it should be at the top and others at the bottom. They decided to prioritize the most important functions, including the power button, channel and volume controls, and the number function. The team also discussed the design of the remote control, including the use of a clip to hold additional buttons and the placement of the buttons to minimize the risk of RSI (repetitive strain injury). The meeting concluded with the team deciding to continue the discussion in the next meeting and to make some decisions on the design and features of the remote control."}
{"input": "Summarize the whole meeting.", "answers": ["The meeting was mainly about conceptual design. In the beginning, the group focused on industrial components: energy, material and interface. The industrial designer preferred traditional batteries, titanium and simple push buttons so that they would have enough money for speech recognition. After that, the group talked about LCD display and locating methods. The second topic was user interface concept, which was basically a good-looking design with few buttons. The group decided to change the colour of the assembly at an extra charge to meet special needs. To make a smart controller, they mentioned speech recognition and user identification. When it came to trend watching, the marketing put forward three noteworthy aspects - being fancy, technologically innovative and useful. The group agreed to follow the trend but the specific design was not decided."], "length": 6952, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting was a conceptual design meeting for a new remote control, where the team discussed various aspects of the design, including the component concept, user interface, and trends in remote control design. The team decided to use a simple battery instead of solar cells, titanium instead of rubber, and push buttons instead of a liquid crystal display (LCD). They also discussed the importance of making the remote control \"fancy\" and \"technologically innovative,\" and considered incorporating features such as speech recognition, color-changing capabilities, and a spongy or fruit-like design. The team assigned tasks to each member, including Rama to design the look and feel, Mark to design the user interface, and Sammy to evaluate the product. They also decided to use modeling clay to create a prototype and will have a follow-up meeting in 30 minutes to discuss their progress."}
{"input": "What were the problems encountered with SmartKom system?", "answers": ["The translation of SmartKom to english is in its final stages. The synthesis module will be the last one to do, after the english syntax trees are completed. The system is still buggy and unstable, but it will soon be ready for a demonstration."], "length": 12646, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The SmartKom system had several problems, including issues with the synthesis module, which didn't know the word \"I\" and required a German synthesis module for English sentences. Additionally, the system had trouble with parsing and generation, and the team had to work around these limitations by using a concept-to-speech approach. They also encountered problems with the dialogue manager, which required manual adjustments to work properly."}
{"input": "What were the advantages of the design for the prototype?", "answers": ["According to the technical specifications given by the industrial designer, the product was very flexible because users could add buttons. Users could use the jog wheel to select whichever option on the LCD and do the power control. There was also an LCD control, so users could switch on the LCD display if they wanted to use LCD and use a normal button if they didn't want to use LCD. What's more, the controller had the functions of speech recognition and location finder."], "length": 8560, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The design for the prototype was considered \"fancy\" and had several advantages, including being fully adaptable, having a simple and perfect shape, and being able to change colors on the LCD display. It also had a unique feature of being able to produce electricity from mechanical energy, eliminating the need for a battery. Additionally, the design was considered robust and useful, with a user-friendly interface and a variety of features such as speech recognition, location finding, and a jog wheel. The design also had a unique and attractive feature of having strawberries on the top, which added to its fanciness and appeal."}
{"input": "What did Industrial Designer think of the scroll wheel when presenting the user interface?", "answers": ["When User Interface mentioned about the new developments they would include, Industrial Designer asked about what a spinning wheel with the LCD display was. User Interface explained that it was a wheel but not separate buttons. Marketing supplemented that it was a fast wheel to control over. Industrial Designer agreed on LCD with spinning and would include it into the design."], "length": 8984, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The Industrial Designer thought that the scroll wheel was a good option, but was unsure about its functionality and whether it was suitable for a remote control. He was also unsure about how it would work with the L_C_D_ display, and was later convinced by the Marketing Expert that it was a good idea to have a scroll wheel, but only if it was a spinning wheel that went at a high speed."}
{"input": "What would happen after the demo?", "answers": ["After the demo, the focus of research can switch towards purely scientific goals, including issues on ontology, deep semantic constructions, execution engines etc. Moreover, a new system will be designed for the project and at least some parts of it should be built."], "length": 12644, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "After the demo, the group would shift gears and focus on the scientific aspects of the project, including formalizing the notation, working on constructions in English and German, and developing a construction analyzer. They would also start building a system, with Johno as the point person on the parser and analyzer, and would need to make decisions on ontology and other system-related issues."}
