{"input": "Summarize the discussion about interface concept design.", "answers": ["The team believed that there were always large and many buttons on the standard remote control. To design its unique device, they decided on a pure color one with arrows to eliminate the number of buttons used. They also planned to incorporate the company logo. Moreover, User Interface suggested a set of buttons with its own color to make it recognizable. But this idea was turned down by Project Manager."], "length": 6029, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The discussion about interface concept design centered around the design of a remote control for a television, with the User Interface presenting their concept, which included large and thin buttons, a power button, mute button, channel buttons, and a menu button. The Industrial Designer presented an alternative concept, which included a scroll wheel and a joystick, and the group discussed the pros and cons of each design, including the use of an LCD screen, the placement of buttons, and the overall shape and feel of the remote control. The group ultimately decided to create two concepts: one with an LCD screen and one without, and to use a normal battery and a regular chip."}
{"input": "Summarize the discussion about the presentation on the prototype model.", "answers": ["With a working prototype, the user interface designer displayed the appearance and the texture of the product, which was fruit-like and spongy. As for the material, they still wanted to use titanium because it was the most modern material that they could select. Then the industrial designer gave some technical specifications on the product, including instructions on buttons, LCD display, speech recognition and location finder."], "length": 8560, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The discussion about the presentation on the prototype model began with the Industrial Designer presenting the model and explaining its features, including the remote control, L_C_D_ display, and speech recognition capabilities. The Marketing team member, Sammy, then proposed some criteria to evaluate the prototype, including its fanciness, technology, robustness, usefulness, size and weight, and adaptability. The team members then discussed and voted on each criterion, with the prototype receiving an average score of 2.17 out of 7. The discussion also touched on the prototype's design, including the use of titanium and the inclusion of strawberries as sensors, and the team's overall impression of the prototype's features and functionality."}
{"input": "What did the meeting discuss about universities that had secured funding for investing in their estates?", "answers": ["Bethan Owen took Cardiff University as an example and he believed that in the short term, the reserves of Cardiff would appear as though they have significant cash balances. However, the investing was much restricted and it could not be a long-term solution towards the serious financial stress. To help with this process as well as monitoring the regulated approaches, the government received forecasts, around five-year forecasts which indicated that, for 2018-19, there would be a sector that was roughly in a break-even position, since the income was not increasing, which was the biggest problem. But luckily, they were not likely to lose any of the universities."], "length": 10208, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The meeting discussed how universities that had secured funding for investing in their estates, such as Cardiff University, had built up significant cash balances that were restricted for investment in the estate, and would be utilized over the next two or three years. This was highlighted by Bethan Owen, who noted that the reserves of Cardiff University would appear to have significant cash balances, but these were restricted for investment in the estate."}
{"input": "What was the debate over the situation of long-term care?", "answers": ["The opposition party questioned about sending military groups to provinces in order to fix long-term care. The Prime Minister answered that the government needed to make sure seniors across the country were properly cared for, and they sent in the military to help the provinces. The opposition party also questioned about applying the national standards so that long-term care was governed by the same principles as the Canada Health Act. The Prime Minister elaborated that due the Covid-19, pushing the project had become extremely difficult."], "length": 20272, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The debate over the situation of long-term care centered around the horrific conditions reported by the Canadian Armed Forces in Ontario, including neglect, abuse, and neglect of seniors, with opposition parties calling for a full public inquiry, national standards, and an end to profit in long-term care, while the government acknowledged the need for improvement and committed to working with provinces and territories to address the issue."}
